
VIDEO 1 — Final Script (With Node + Anti-Gravity)

⸻

0:00 – 0:25 — Face Cam

“Today I’m going to show you how simple this actually is.

In the next few minutes, we’ll set up the basic tools, deploy a web app to the internet, change something inside the code, and watch it update live.

No chaos.
No theory.
Just the real workflow.

My name is Steve. I’m a software tester.

I care about things working — clearly and predictably.

Let’s start.”

Hard cut to screen share.

⸻

0:25 – 0:55 — Tools & Assumptions

“Here’s what we’re using today.

GitHub — that’s where your project lives online.
Vercel — that’s what puts your project live on the internet.
Node.js — that allows your computer to run modern web apps.
Anti-Gravity — that’s where we’ll work with the project using AI.

I’m on macOS.
If you’re on Windows, the flow is almost identical.

If you don’t have GitHub yet, pause and create an account. It’s free.

Let’s install Node first.”

⸻

0:55 – 1:40 — Install Node.js (Simple)

Open nodejs.org.

“Go to nodejs.org.

Click the LTS version — that stands for Long Term Support.

Download it.
Install it like any normal application.

That’s it.

You don’t need to understand what Node is yet.
Just know your computer now understands modern JavaScript projects.”

Pause.

“Now let’s open Anti-Gravity.”

⸻

1:40 – 2:20 — Open Anti-Gravity

Open Anti-Gravity.

“This is where I work.

Instead of memorizing terminal commands, I describe what I want.

You’ll see how simple this can be.”

Pause.

“Now let’s deploy something real.”

⸻

2:20 – 3:40 — Deploy Boilerplate on Vercel

Open vercel.com.

“Log into Vercel.

Click ‘Add New Project.’

We’ll use a template — just a clean starting point.”

Select Next.js starter.

“This is an empty boilerplate.
That’s intentional.”

Click Deploy.

“We’re not configuring servers.
We’re not touching infrastructure.
We’re just deploying.”

Pause during build.

“You don’t need a developer for this.
You just need to understand the flow.”

Deployment completes.

Open live URL.

“And this is now live on the internet.”

Pause.

⸻

3:40 – 4:30 — Show GitHub Repo

Back to Vercel dashboard.

“Vercel automatically created a GitHub repository for this project.”

Open repository.

“This is the source code behind the site.

This is where we make changes.”

⸻

4:30 – 5:40 — Edit Code in GitHub

Open homepage file.

“I’m going to edit this file directly inside GitHub.”

Click pencil icon.

“I’ll change one line of text.”

Edit headline.

Scroll down.

“Now I commit the change.

A commit is just saving a new version.”

Click Commit.

“That’s it.”

⸻

5:40 – 6:20 — Automatic Redeploy

Switch back to Vercel.

“Now watch this.

Because Vercel is connected to GitHub, it automatically detects the change.”

Show deployment starting.

“Every update triggers a rebuild.”

Pause.

Deployment completes.

Refresh live site.

“And the change is live.”

⸻

6:20 – 6:45 — Confidence & Framing

“Pause for a second.

You installed the basic tools.
You deployed a project.
You edited the code.
You redeployed automatically.

This is the baseline.

Once you understand this loop, you’re no longer dependent on someone else for basic deployment.”

⸻

6:45 – 7:00 — CTA + Preview

“If you’re serious about building this the right way, start with The SaaS Starting Point.

It’s free, and it helps you avoid building in the wrong order.

In the next video, I’ll show you what usually breaks after this step — and how to prevent it.”

End.
